    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some walnut dovetails for a cabinet commission and I'm having a recurring issue. The pins are splintering right at the tips during the final paring. I've sharpened my chisels to a mirror finish, but the fibers seem to be tearing instead of shearing cleanly. Any thoughts on whether this is a grain direction issue or if I need to look at my moisture content/tool angle?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This splintering often points to a combination of factors, but it's frequently related to the walnut's grain structure. Since walnut can have notoriously figured or hard grain, tearing is common when the blade engages at an improper angle, causing it to rip fibers rather than slice them. Try adjusting your chisel angle to be slightly more acute, or experiment with very light, careful draw cuts to establish a clean baseline before finalizing the shape.",
      "source": "target",
