    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've tried the draw cut approach, but it hasn't really fixed the issue. The splintering happens even on the most controlled passes. I've also checked my chisel bevels—they're definitely sharp enough. Could it be something about the wood itself, like the density or how it's reacting to the workshop humidity? I'm starting to feel like the wood is just being difficult for no reason.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is possible the wood itself is the primary hurdle, especially if that walnut is dense or has an unevenly stressed growth pattern. High humidity swings can also cause localized swelling or shrinkage, making the grain unpredictable during paring. Consider running a small sample block of the same wood through the same process to definitively isolate whether the issue is consistent with the material versus the technique.",
      "source": "target",
