    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I did run a test block and it's doing the exact same thing. It's really frustrating because everything seems perfect on my end. It almost feels like the wood is resisting on purpose, like it's intentionally sabotaging the joints just as I'm getting close to the finish. Do you think there's any way the internal tension in the timber could be causing this kind of sudden, localized failure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, internal tension within the timber, often due to drying stresses before milling, can absolutely cause these stubborn tearing failures. This residual stress acts like a spring, causing localized fibers to fail catastrophically under pressure rather than yielding smoothly. In these cases, slightly reducing the pressure and making multiple, extremely shallow passes is often the only way to overcome the internal resistance without forcing the chisel through the stress point.",
      "source": "target",
